1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,930


2
00:00:00,930 --> 00:00:04,050
Welcome to the first lecture
of this class.

3
00:00:04,050 --> 00:00:06,860
You may be used to having a
first lecture devoted to

4
00:00:06,860 --> 00:00:09,670
general comments and motivating
examples.

5
00:00:09,670 --> 00:00:11,020
This one will be different.

6
00:00:11,020 --> 00:00:14,500
We will dive into the heart
of the subject right away.

7
00:00:14,500 --> 00:00:16,920
In fact, today we will
accomplish a lot.

8
00:00:16,920 --> 00:00:20,030
By the end of this lecture, you
will know about all of the

9
00:00:20,030 --> 00:00:23,120
elements of a probabilistic
model.

10
00:00:23,120 --> 00:00:26,060
A probabilistic model is a
quantitative description of a

11
00:00:26,060 --> 00:00:29,080
situation, a phenomenon,
or an experiment

12
00:00:29,080 --> 00:00:31,320
whose outcome is uncertain.

13
00:00:31,320 --> 00:00:34,850
Putting together such a model
involves two key steps.

14
00:00:34,850 --> 00:00:38,140
First, we need to describe the
possible outcomes of the

15
00:00:38,140 --> 00:00:39,320
experiment.

16
00:00:39,320 --> 00:00:42,910
This is done by specifying
a so-called sample space.

17
00:00:42,910 --> 00:00:46,170
And then, we specify a
probability law, which assigns

18
00:00:46,170 --> 00:00:50,060
probabilities to outcomes or
to collections of outcomes.

19
00:00:50,060 --> 00:00:53,360
The probability law tells us,
for example, whether one

20
00:00:53,360 --> 00:00:57,110
outcome is much more likely
than some other outcome.

21
00:00:57,110 --> 00:01:00,640
Probabilities have to satisfy
certain basic properties in

22
00:01:00,640 --> 00:01:02,070
order to be meaningful.

23
00:01:02,070 --> 00:01:05,069
These are the axioms of
probability theory.

24
00:01:05,069 --> 00:01:08,370
For example probabilities
cannot be negative.

25
00:01:08,370 --> 00:01:11,940
Interestingly, there will be
very few axioms, but they are

26
00:01:11,940 --> 00:01:14,890
powerful, and we will see
that they have lots of

27
00:01:14,890 --> 00:01:16,220
consequences.

28
00:01:16,220 --> 00:01:19,400
We will see that they imply
many other properties that

29
00:01:19,400 --> 00:01:22,480
were not part of the axioms.

30
00:01:22,480 --> 00:01:25,510
We will then go through a couple
of very simple examples

31
00:01:25,510 --> 00:01:27,790
involving models with
either discrete

32
00:01:27,790 --> 00:01:29,730
or continuous outcomes.

33
00:01:29,730 --> 00:01:32,690
As you will be seeing many times
in this class, discrete

34
00:01:32,690 --> 00:01:35,070
models are conceptually
much easier.

35
00:01:35,070 --> 00:01:39,160
Continuous models involve some
more sophisticated concepts,

36
00:01:39,160 --> 00:01:43,270
and we will point out some of
the subtle issues that arise.

37
00:01:43,270 --> 00:01:45,880
And finally, we will talk a
little bit about the big

38
00:01:45,880 --> 00:01:49,560
picture, about the role of
probability theory, and its

39
00:01:49,560 --> 00:01:51,220
relation with the real world.

40
00:01:51,220 --> 00:01:52,470


41
00:01:51,645 --> 00:01:54,945


42
00:01:54,945 --> 00:01:57,545
Putting together a probabilistic
model--

43
00:01:57,545 --> 00:02:00,705
that is, a model of a random
phenomenon or a random

44
00:02:00,705 --> 00:02:01,945
experiment--

45
00:02:01,945 --> 00:02:04,005
involves two steps.

46
00:02:04,005 --> 00:02:07,665
First step, we describe the
possible outcomes of the

47
00:02:07,665 --> 00:02:10,575
phenomenon or experiment
of interest.

48
00:02:10,575 --> 00:02:14,585
Second step, we describe our
beliefs about the likelihood

49
00:02:14,585 --> 00:02:17,305
of the different possible
outcomes by specifying a

50
00:02:17,305 --> 00:02:19,215
probability law.

51
00:02:19,215 --> 00:02:23,295
Here, we start by just talking
about the first step, namely,

52
00:02:23,295 --> 00:02:25,475
the description of the possible
outcomes of the

53
00:02:25,475 --> 00:02:26,595
experiment.

54
00:02:26,595 --> 00:02:28,405
So we carry out an experiment.

55
00:02:28,405 --> 00:02:30,185
For example, we flip a coin.

56
00:02:30,185 --> 00:02:33,045
Or maybe we flip five coins
simultaneously.

57
00:02:33,045 --> 00:02:35,385
Or maybe we roll a die.

58
00:02:35,385 --> 00:02:40,035
Whatever that experiment is,
it has a number of possible

59
00:02:40,035 --> 00:02:43,615
outcomes, and we start
by making a list of

60
00:02:43,615 --> 00:02:45,475
the possible outcomes--

61
00:02:45,475 --> 00:02:49,135
or, a better word, instead of
the word "list", is to use the

62
00:02:49,135 --> 00:02:53,165
word "set", which has a more
formal mathematical meaning.

63
00:02:53,165 --> 00:02:57,045
So we create a set
that we usually

64
00:02:57,045 --> 00:03:01,495
denote by capital omega.

65
00:03:01,495 --> 00:03:06,215
That set is called the sample
space and is the set of all

66
00:03:06,215 --> 00:03:09,210
possible outcomes of
our experiment.

67
00:03:09,210 --> 00:03:12,935


68
00:03:12,935 --> 00:03:16,395
The elements of that set
should have certain

69
00:03:16,395 --> 00:03:17,625
properties.

70
00:03:17,625 --> 00:03:21,235
Namely, the elements should
be mutually exclusive and

71
00:03:21,235 --> 00:03:23,265
collectively exhaustive.

72
00:03:23,265 --> 00:03:24,505
What does that mean?

73
00:03:24,505 --> 00:03:27,935
Mutually exclusive means that,
if at the end of the

74
00:03:27,935 --> 00:03:32,805
experiment, I tell you that this
outcome happened, then it

75
00:03:32,805 --> 00:03:36,615
should not be possible that this
outcome also happened.

76
00:03:36,615 --> 00:03:39,405
At the end of the experiment,
there can only be one of the

77
00:03:39,405 --> 00:03:41,845
outcomes that has happened.

78
00:03:41,845 --> 00:03:45,065
Being collectively exhaustive
means something else-- that,

79
00:03:45,065 --> 00:03:48,975
together, all of these elements
of the set exhaust

80
00:03:48,975 --> 00:03:51,045
all the possibilities.

81
00:03:51,045 --> 00:03:55,045
So no matter what, at the end,
you will be able to point to

82
00:03:55,045 --> 00:03:59,505
one of the outcomes and say,
that's the one that occurred.

83
00:03:59,505 --> 00:04:00,654
To summarize--

84
00:04:00,654 --> 00:04:03,985
this set should be such that, at
the end of the experiment,

85
00:04:03,985 --> 00:04:08,915
you should be always able to
point to one, and exactly one,

86
00:04:08,915 --> 00:04:12,305
of the possible outcomes and say
that this is the outcome

87
00:04:12,305 --> 00:04:13,555
that occurred.

88
00:04:13,555 --> 00:04:15,345


89
00:04:15,345 --> 00:04:20,515
Physically different outcomes
should be distinguished in the

90
00:04:20,515 --> 00:04:25,175
sample space and correspond
to distinct points.

91
00:04:25,175 --> 00:04:27,405
But when we say physically
different

92
00:04:27,405 --> 00:04:29,485
outcomes, what do we mean?

93
00:04:29,485 --> 00:04:33,555
We really mean different in
all relevant aspects but

94
00:04:33,555 --> 00:04:37,525
perhaps not different in
irrelevant aspects.

95
00:04:37,525 --> 00:04:41,825
Let's make more precise what I
mean by that by looking at a

96
00:04:41,825 --> 00:04:45,245
very simple, and maybe
silly, example,

97
00:04:45,245 --> 00:04:46,270
which is the following.

98
00:04:46,270 --> 00:04:50,005
Suppose that you flip a coin
and you see whether it

99
00:04:50,005 --> 00:04:53,625
resulted in heads or tails.

100
00:04:53,625 --> 00:04:57,535
So you have a perfectly
legitimate sample space for

101
00:04:57,535 --> 00:05:01,115
this experiment which consists
of just two points--

102
00:05:01,115 --> 00:05:02,845
heads and tails.

103
00:05:02,845 --> 00:05:08,395
Together these two outcomes
exhaust all possibilities.

104
00:05:08,395 --> 00:05:11,025
And the two outcomes are
mutually exclusive.

105
00:05:11,025 --> 00:05:13,845
So this is a very legitimate
sample space for this

106
00:05:13,845 --> 00:05:15,405
experiment.

107
00:05:15,405 --> 00:05:18,265
Now suppose that while you were
flipping the coin, you

108
00:05:18,265 --> 00:05:21,755
also looked outside the window
to check the weather.

109
00:05:21,755 --> 00:05:28,785
And then you could say that my
sample space is really, heads,

110
00:05:28,785 --> 00:05:30,055
and it's raining.

111
00:05:30,055 --> 00:05:32,615


112
00:05:32,615 --> 00:05:36,610
Another possible outcome
is heads and no rain.

113
00:05:36,610 --> 00:05:40,425


114
00:05:40,425 --> 00:05:47,285
Another possible outcome is
tails, and it's raining, and,

115
00:05:47,285 --> 00:05:51,620
finally, another possible
outcome is tails and no rain.

116
00:05:51,620 --> 00:05:57,135


117
00:05:57,135 --> 00:06:03,205
This set, consisting of four
elements, is also a perfectly

118
00:06:03,205 --> 00:06:05,960
legitimate sample space
for the experiment

119
00:06:05,960 --> 00:06:07,785
of flipping a coin.

120
00:06:07,785 --> 00:06:10,705
The elements of this sample
space are mutually exclusive

121
00:06:10,705 --> 00:06:12,035
and collectively exhaustive.

122
00:06:12,035 --> 00:06:16,475
Exactly one of these outcomes
is going to be true, or will

123
00:06:16,475 --> 00:06:19,285
have materialized, at the
end of the experiment.

124
00:06:19,285 --> 00:06:22,085
So which sample space
is the correct one?

125
00:06:22,085 --> 00:06:24,595
This sample space, the
second one, involves

126
00:06:24,595 --> 00:06:26,615
some irrelevant details.

127
00:06:26,615 --> 00:06:31,735
So the preferred sample space
for describing the flipping of

128
00:06:31,735 --> 00:06:35,905
a coin, the preferred sample
space is the simpler one, the

129
00:06:35,905 --> 00:06:39,985
first one, which is sort of at
the right granularity, given

130
00:06:39,985 --> 00:06:42,285
what we're interested in.

131
00:06:42,285 --> 00:06:45,655
But ultimately, the question
of which one is the right

132
00:06:45,655 --> 00:06:48,405
sample space depends
on what kind of

133
00:06:48,405 --> 00:06:50,485
questions you want to answer.

134
00:06:50,485 --> 00:06:53,925
For example, if you have a
theory that the weather

135
00:06:53,925 --> 00:06:58,665
affects the behavior of coins,
then, in order to play with

136
00:06:58,665 --> 00:07:03,605
that theory, or maybe check it
out, and so on, then, in such

137
00:07:03,605 --> 00:07:07,455
a case, you might want to work
with the second sample space.

138
00:07:07,455 --> 00:07:10,715
This is a common feature
in all of science.

139
00:07:10,715 --> 00:07:14,315
Whenever you put together a
model, you need to decide how

140
00:07:14,315 --> 00:07:16,725
detailed you want your
model to be.

141
00:07:16,725 --> 00:07:20,515
And the right level of detail is
the one that captures those

142
00:07:20,515 --> 00:07:24,145
aspects that are relevant
and of interest to you.

143
00:07:24,145 --> 00:07:29,478


144
00:07:29,518 --> 00:07:32,178


145
00:07:32,178 --> 00:07:35,788
Let us now look at some examples
of sample spaces.

146
00:07:35,788 --> 00:07:38,158
Sample spaces are sets.

147
00:07:38,158 --> 00:07:41,948
And a set can be discrete,
finite, infinite,

148
00:07:41,948 --> 00:07:43,398
continuous, and so on.

149
00:07:43,398 --> 00:07:47,248
Let us start with a simpler case
in which we have a sample

150
00:07:47,248 --> 00:07:50,138
space that is discrete
and finite.

151
00:07:50,138 --> 00:07:52,448
The particular experiment
we will be

152
00:07:52,448 --> 00:07:54,858
looking at is the following.

153
00:07:54,858 --> 00:07:58,818
We take a very special die,
a tetrahedral die.

154
00:07:58,818 --> 00:08:04,108
So it's a die that has four
faces numbered from 1 up 4.

155
00:08:04,108 --> 00:08:06,248
We roll it once.

156
00:08:06,248 --> 00:08:08,798
And then we roll it
twice [again].

157
00:08:08,798 --> 00:08:11,608
Were not dealing here with two
probabilistic experiments.

158
00:08:11,608 --> 00:08:14,948
We're dealing with a single
probabilistic experiment that

159
00:08:14,948 --> 00:08:19,638
involves two rolls of the die
within that experiment.

160
00:08:19,638 --> 00:08:22,478
What is the sample space
of that experiment?

161
00:08:22,478 --> 00:08:23,688
Well, one possible

162
00:08:23,688 --> 00:08:26,288
representation is the following.

163
00:08:26,288 --> 00:08:29,998
We take note of the result
of the first roll.

164
00:08:29,998 --> 00:08:33,738
And then we take note of the
result of the second roll.

165
00:08:33,738 --> 00:08:36,568
And this gives us a
pair of numbers.

166
00:08:36,568 --> 00:08:40,468
Each one of the possible pairs
of numbers corresponds to one

167
00:08:40,468 --> 00:08:42,968
of the little squares
in this diagram.

168
00:08:42,968 --> 00:08:47,798
For example, if the first roll
is 1 and the second is also 1,

169
00:08:47,798 --> 00:08:51,508
then this particular outcome
has occurred.

170
00:08:51,508 --> 00:08:56,578
If the first roll is it 2 and
the second is a 3, then this

171
00:08:56,578 --> 00:08:59,298
particular outcome occurs.

172
00:08:59,298 --> 00:09:03,468
If the first roll is a 3 and
then the next one is a 2, then

173
00:09:03,468 --> 00:09:06,098
this particular outcome
occurs.

174
00:09:06,098 --> 00:09:09,768
Notice that these two outcomes
are pretty closely related.

175
00:09:09,768 --> 00:09:13,358
In both cases, we observe
a 2 and we observe a 3.

176
00:09:13,358 --> 00:09:17,568
But we distinguish those two
outcomes because in those two

177
00:09:17,568 --> 00:09:22,018
outcomes, the 2 and the 3 happen
in different order.

178
00:09:22,018 --> 00:09:25,608
And the order in which they
appear may be a detail which

179
00:09:25,608 --> 00:09:27,078
is of interest to us.

180
00:09:27,078 --> 00:09:29,138
And so we make this distinction

181
00:09:29,138 --> 00:09:30,528
in the sample space.

182
00:09:30,528 --> 00:09:38,458
So we keep the (3, 2) and the
(2, 3) as separate outcomes.

183
00:09:38,458 --> 00:09:41,968
Now this is a case of a model
in which the probabilistic

184
00:09:41,968 --> 00:09:46,688
experiment can be described
in phases or stages.

185
00:09:46,688 --> 00:09:51,758
We could think about rolling
the die once and then going

186
00:09:51,758 --> 00:09:53,338
ahead with the second roll.

187
00:09:53,338 --> 00:09:55,238
So we have two stages.

188
00:09:55,238 --> 00:09:58,408
A very useful way of describing
the sample space of

189
00:09:58,408 --> 00:09:59,468
experiments--

190
00:09:59,468 --> 00:10:04,908
whenever we have an experiment
with several stages, either

191
00:10:04,908 --> 00:10:07,298
real stages or imagined
stages.

192
00:10:07,298 --> 00:10:10,498
So a very useful way of
describing it is by providing

193
00:10:10,498 --> 00:10:14,588
a sequential description
in terms of a tree.

194
00:10:14,588 --> 00:10:18,758
So a diagram of this kind,
we call it a tree.

195
00:10:18,758 --> 00:10:21,288
You can think of this as
the root of the tree

196
00:10:21,288 --> 00:10:22,808
from which you start.

197
00:10:22,808 --> 00:10:26,288
And the endpoints of the
tree, we usually

198
00:10:26,288 --> 00:10:28,298
call them the leaves.

199
00:10:28,298 --> 00:10:30,078
So the experiment starts.

200
00:10:30,078 --> 00:10:33,028
We carry out the first phase,
which in this case is the

201
00:10:33,028 --> 00:10:34,138
first roll.

202
00:10:34,138 --> 00:10:35,428
And we see what happens.

203
00:10:35,428 --> 00:10:39,088
So maybe we get a 2
in the first roll.

204
00:10:39,088 --> 00:10:43,398
And then we take note of what
happened in the second roll.

205
00:10:43,398 --> 00:10:46,358
And maybe the result was a 3.

206
00:10:46,358 --> 00:10:48,238
So we follow this branch here.

207
00:10:48,238 --> 00:10:52,128
And we end up at this particular
leaf, which is the

208
00:10:52,128 --> 00:10:55,548
leaf associated with
the outcome 2, 3.

209
00:10:55,548 --> 00:10:59,278
Notice that in this tree we once
more have a distinction.

210
00:10:59,278 --> 00:11:03,258
The outcome 2 followed by a 3 is
different from the outcome

211
00:11:03,258 --> 00:11:07,708
3 followed by a 2, which would
correspond to this particular

212
00:11:07,708 --> 00:11:10,408
place in the diagram.

213
00:11:10,408 --> 00:11:14,028
In both cases, we have
16 possible outcomes.

214
00:11:14,028 --> 00:11:15,878
4 times 4 makes 16.

215
00:11:15,878 --> 00:11:19,038
And similarly, if you count
here, the number of leaves is

216
00:11:19,038 --> 00:11:21,748
equal to 16.

217
00:11:21,748 --> 00:11:25,188
The previous example involves
a sample space that was

218
00:11:25,188 --> 00:11:27,028
discrete and finite.

219
00:11:27,028 --> 00:11:29,958
There were only 16 possible
outcomes.

220
00:11:29,958 --> 00:11:33,168
But sample spaces can
also be infinite.

221
00:11:33,168 --> 00:11:35,958
And they could also be
continuous sets.

222
00:11:35,958 --> 00:11:39,048
Here's an example of an
experiment that involves a

223
00:11:39,048 --> 00:11:41,158
continuous sample space.

224
00:11:41,158 --> 00:11:43,648
So we have a rectangular target

225
00:11:43,648 --> 00:11:46,558
which is the unit square.

226
00:11:46,558 --> 00:11:49,857
And you throw a dart
on that target.

227
00:11:49,857 --> 00:11:54,718
And suppose that you are so
skilled that no matter what,

228
00:11:54,718 --> 00:11:57,148
when you throw the
dart, it always

229
00:11:57,148 --> 00:12:00,858
falls inside the target.

230
00:12:00,858 --> 00:12:07,488
Once the dart hits the target,
you record the coordinates x

231
00:12:07,488 --> 00:12:11,448
and y of the particular
point that resulted

232
00:12:11,448 --> 00:12:13,048
from your dart throw.

233
00:12:13,048 --> 00:12:17,898
And we record x and y with
infinite precision.

234
00:12:17,898 --> 00:12:20,638
So x and y are real numbers.

235
00:12:20,638 --> 00:12:24,538
So in this experiment, the
sample space is just the set

236
00:12:24,538 --> 00:12:29,708
of x, y pairs that lie
between 0 and 1.

237
00:12:29,708 --> 00:12:31,985


238
00:12:32,028 --> 00:12:33,528


239
00:12:33,528 --> 00:12:36,968
We have so far discussed the
first step involved in the

240
00:12:36,968 --> 00:12:39,378
construction of a probabilistic
model.

241
00:12:39,378 --> 00:12:42,958
Namely, the construction of
a sample space, which is a

242
00:12:42,958 --> 00:12:45,758
description of the possible
outcomes of a probabilistic

243
00:12:45,758 --> 00:12:47,128
experiment.

244
00:12:47,128 --> 00:12:51,788
We now come to the second and
much more interesting part.

245
00:12:51,788 --> 00:12:56,238
We need to specify which
outcomes are more likely to

246
00:12:56,238 --> 00:13:00,568
occur and which ones are less
likely to occur and so on.

247
00:13:00,568 --> 00:13:05,308
And we will do that by assigning
probabilities to the

248
00:13:05,308 --> 00:13:07,248
different outcomes.

249
00:13:07,248 --> 00:13:12,578
However, as we try to do this
assignment, we run into some

250
00:13:12,578 --> 00:13:15,368
kind of difficulty, which
is the following.

251
00:13:15,368 --> 00:13:18,338
Remember the previous experiment
involving a

252
00:13:18,338 --> 00:13:22,908
continuous sample space, which
was the unit square and in

253
00:13:22,908 --> 00:13:27,308
which we throw a dart at random
and record the point

254
00:13:27,308 --> 00:13:29,188
that occurred.

255
00:13:29,188 --> 00:13:32,788
In this experiment, what do you
think is the probability

256
00:13:32,788 --> 00:13:34,888
of a particular point?

257
00:13:34,888 --> 00:13:39,568
Let's say what is the
probability that my dart hits

258
00:13:39,568 --> 00:13:43,918
exactly the center
of this square.

259
00:13:43,918 --> 00:13:46,358
Well, this probability would
be essentially 0.

260
00:13:46,358 --> 00:13:49,018
Hitting the center exactly
with infinite

261
00:13:49,018 --> 00:13:50,898
precision should be 0.

262
00:13:50,898 --> 00:13:54,488
And so it's natural that in such
a continuous model any

263
00:13:54,488 --> 00:13:59,048
individual point should
have a 0 probability.

264
00:13:59,048 --> 00:14:03,308
For this reason instead of
assigning probabilities to

265
00:14:03,308 --> 00:14:08,668
individual points, we will
instead assign probabilities

266
00:14:08,668 --> 00:14:14,898
to whole sets, that is, to
subsets of the sample space.

267
00:14:14,898 --> 00:14:21,118
So here we have our sample
space, which is some

268
00:14:21,118 --> 00:14:25,648
abstract set omega.

269
00:14:25,648 --> 00:14:28,598
Here is a subset of
the sample space.

270
00:14:28,598 --> 00:14:33,568
Call it capital A. We're going
to assign a probability to

271
00:14:33,568 --> 00:14:37,648
that subset A, which we're
going to denote with this

272
00:14:37,648 --> 00:14:44,408
notation, which we read as the
probability of set A. So

273
00:14:44,408 --> 00:14:47,608
probabilities will be
assigned to subsets.

274
00:14:47,608 --> 00:14:50,448
And these will not cause us
difficulties in the continuous

275
00:14:50,448 --> 00:14:54,308
case because even though
individual points would have 0

276
00:14:54,308 --> 00:14:58,748
probability, if you ask me what
are the odds that my dart

277
00:14:58,748 --> 00:15:03,788
falls in the upper half, let's
say, of this diagram, then

278
00:15:03,788 --> 00:15:06,868
that should be a reasonable
positive number.

279
00:15:06,868 --> 00:15:09,248
So even though individual
outcomes may have 0

280
00:15:09,248 --> 00:15:13,138
probabilities, sets of outcomes
in general would be

281
00:15:13,138 --> 00:15:16,258
expected to have positive
probabilities.

282
00:15:16,258 --> 00:15:20,828
So coming back, we're going to
assign probabilities to the

283
00:15:20,828 --> 00:15:23,838
various subsets of
the sample space.

284
00:15:23,838 --> 00:15:27,118
And here comes a piece of
terminology, that a subset of

285
00:15:27,118 --> 00:15:29,918
the sample space is
called an event.

286
00:15:29,918 --> 00:15:31,908
Why is it called an event?

287
00:15:31,908 --> 00:15:36,138
Because once we carry out the
experiment and we observe the

288
00:15:36,138 --> 00:15:40,178
outcome of the experiment,
either this outcome is inside

289
00:15:40,178 --> 00:15:45,848
the set A and in that case we
say that event A has occurred,

290
00:15:45,848 --> 00:15:50,578
or the outcome falls outside the
set A in which case we say

291
00:15:50,578 --> 00:15:54,668
that event A did not occur.

292
00:15:54,668 --> 00:15:58,658
Now we want to move on and
describe certain rules.

293
00:15:58,658 --> 00:16:01,368
The rules of the game in
probabilistic models, which

294
00:16:01,368 --> 00:16:03,598
are basically the
rules that these

295
00:16:03,598 --> 00:16:06,058
probabilities should satisfy.

296
00:16:06,058 --> 00:16:08,538
They shouldn't be completely
arbitrary.

297
00:16:08,538 --> 00:16:14,268
First, by convention,
probabilities are always given

298
00:16:14,268 --> 00:16:17,298
in the range between 0 and 1.

299
00:16:17,298 --> 00:16:20,418
Intuitively, 0 probability means
that we believe that

300
00:16:20,418 --> 00:16:23,278
something practically
cannot happen.

301
00:16:23,278 --> 00:16:28,708
And probability of 1 means that
we're practically certain

302
00:16:28,708 --> 00:16:32,108
that an event of interest
is going to happen.

303
00:16:32,108 --> 00:16:36,438
So we want to specify rules of
these kind for probabilities.

304
00:16:36,438 --> 00:16:39,268
These rules that any
probabilistic model should

305
00:16:39,268 --> 00:16:43,128
satisfy are called the axioms
of probability theory.

306
00:16:43,128 --> 00:16:46,588
And our first axiom is a
nonnegativity axiom.

307
00:16:46,588 --> 00:16:48,838
Namely, probabilities
will always be

308
00:16:48,838 --> 00:16:51,158
non-negative numbers.

309
00:16:51,158 --> 00:16:52,968
It's a reasonable rule.

310
00:16:52,968 --> 00:16:58,078
The second rule is that if the
subset that we're looking at

311
00:16:58,078 --> 00:17:01,788
is actually not a subset but
is the entire sample space

312
00:17:01,788 --> 00:17:07,838
omega, the probability of it
should always be equal to 1.

313
00:17:07,838 --> 00:17:09,598
What does that mean?

314
00:17:09,598 --> 00:17:12,878
We know that the outcome is
going to be an element of the

315
00:17:12,878 --> 00:17:13,878
sample space.

316
00:17:13,878 --> 00:17:16,458
This is the definition
of the sample space.

317
00:17:16,458 --> 00:17:20,028
So we have absolute certainty
that our outcome is going to

318
00:17:20,028 --> 00:17:21,518
be in omega.

319
00:17:21,518 --> 00:17:24,688
Or in different language we have
absolute certainty that

320
00:17:24,688 --> 00:17:27,658
event omega is going to occur.

321
00:17:27,658 --> 00:17:31,198
And we capture this certainty by
saying that the probability

322
00:17:31,198 --> 00:17:34,788
of event omega is equal to 1.

323
00:17:34,788 --> 00:17:39,058
These two axioms are pretty
simple and very intuitive.

324
00:17:39,058 --> 00:17:43,178
The more interesting axiom
is the next one that says

325
00:17:43,178 --> 00:17:45,908
something a little
more complicated.

326
00:17:45,908 --> 00:17:50,828
Before we discuss that
particular axiom, a quick

327
00:17:50,828 --> 00:17:54,798
reminder about set theoretic
notation.

328
00:17:54,798 --> 00:18:01,278
If we have two sets, let's say
a set A, and another set,

329
00:18:01,278 --> 00:18:10,288
another set B, we use this
particular notation, which we

330
00:18:10,288 --> 00:18:16,388
read as "A intersection B" to
refer to the collection of

331
00:18:16,388 --> 00:18:21,988
elements that belong to both A
and B. So in this picture, the

332
00:18:21,988 --> 00:18:28,418
intersection of A and B
is this shaded set.

333
00:18:28,418 --> 00:18:35,058
We use this notation, which we
read as "A union B", to refer

334
00:18:35,058 --> 00:18:38,988
to the set of elements
that belong to A

335
00:18:38,988 --> 00:18:41,868
or to B or to both.

336
00:18:41,868 --> 00:18:46,018
So in terms of this picture,
the union of the two sets

337
00:18:46,018 --> 00:18:49,278
would be this blue set.

338
00:18:49,278 --> 00:18:52,858
After this reminder about set
theoretic notation, now let us

339
00:18:52,858 --> 00:18:55,398
look at the form of
the third axiom.

340
00:18:55,398 --> 00:18:56,908
What does it say?

341
00:18:56,908 --> 00:19:00,888
If we have two sets, two events,
two subsets of the

342
00:19:00,888 --> 00:19:05,508
sample space, which
are disjoint.

343
00:19:05,508 --> 00:19:08,488
So here's our sample space.

344
00:19:08,488 --> 00:19:12,248
And here are the two sets
that are disjoint.

345
00:19:12,248 --> 00:19:15,368


346
00:19:15,368 --> 00:19:19,138
In mathematical terms, two sets
being disjoint means that

347
00:19:19,138 --> 00:19:22,668
their intersection
has no elements.

348
00:19:22,668 --> 00:19:25,648
So their intersection
is the empty set.

349
00:19:25,648 --> 00:19:31,188
And we use this symbol here
to denote the empty set.

350
00:19:31,188 --> 00:19:35,058
So if the intersection of two
sets is empty, then the

351
00:19:35,058 --> 00:19:39,713
probability that the outcome
of the experiments falls in

352
00:19:39,713 --> 00:19:43,768
the union of A and B, that is,
the probability that the

353
00:19:43,768 --> 00:19:49,288
outcome is here or there, is
equal to the sum of the

354
00:19:49,288 --> 00:19:53,728
probabilities of
these two sets.

355
00:19:53,728 --> 00:19:56,098
This is called the
additivity axiom.

356
00:19:56,098 --> 00:20:01,068
So it says that we can add
probabilities of different

357
00:20:01,068 --> 00:20:04,788
sets when those two
sets are disjoint.

358
00:20:04,788 --> 00:20:10,288
In some sense we can think of
probability as being one pound

359
00:20:10,288 --> 00:20:15,038
of some substance which is
spread over our sample space

360
00:20:15,038 --> 00:20:18,808
and the probability of A is how
much of that substance is

361
00:20:18,808 --> 00:20:23,688
sitting on top of a set A. So
what this axiom is saying is

362
00:20:23,688 --> 00:20:28,768
that the total amount of that
substance sitting on top of A

363
00:20:28,768 --> 00:20:33,538
and B is how much is sitting on
top of A plus how much is

364
00:20:33,538 --> 00:20:37,618
sitting on top of B. And that is
the case whenever the sets

365
00:20:37,618 --> 00:20:42,538
A and B are disjoint
from each other.

366
00:20:42,538 --> 00:20:45,988
The additivity axiom needs
to be refined a bit.

367
00:20:45,988 --> 00:20:48,684
We will talk about that
a little later.

368
00:20:48,684 --> 00:20:52,178
Other than this refinement,
these three axioms are the

369
00:20:52,178 --> 00:20:54,518
only requirements in
order to have a

370
00:20:54,518 --> 00:20:57,128
legitimate probability model.

371
00:20:57,128 --> 00:20:59,978
At this point you may ask,
shouldn't there be more

372
00:20:59,978 --> 00:21:01,298
requirements?

373
00:21:01,298 --> 00:21:04,937
Shouldn't we, for example, say
that probabilities cannot be

374
00:21:04,937 --> 00:21:06,978
greater than 1?

375
00:21:06,978 --> 00:21:08,788
Yes and no.

376
00:21:08,788 --> 00:21:12,598
We do not want probabilities to
be larger than 1, but we do

377
00:21:12,598 --> 00:21:14,348
not need to say it.

378
00:21:14,348 --> 00:21:17,488
As we will see in the next
segment, such a requirement

379
00:21:17,488 --> 00:21:20,368
follows from what we
have already said.

380
00:21:20,368 --> 00:21:23,798
And the same is true for
several other natural

381
00:21:23,798 --> 00:21:25,138
properties of probabilities.

382
00:21:25,138 --> 00:21:26,528


383
00:21:26,575 --> 00:21:29,125


384
00:21:29,125 --> 00:21:31,575
The probability axioms
are the basic rules

385
00:21:31,575 --> 00:21:33,285
of probability theory.

386
00:21:33,285 --> 00:21:35,805
And they are surprisingly few.

387
00:21:35,805 --> 00:21:38,165
But they imply many interesting
properties that we

388
00:21:38,165 --> 00:21:39,655
will now explore.

389
00:21:39,655 --> 00:21:43,485
First we will see that what you
might think of as missing

390
00:21:43,485 --> 00:21:48,495
axioms are actually implied by
the axioms already in place.

391
00:21:48,495 --> 00:21:53,425
For example, we have an axiom
that probabilities are

392
00:21:53,425 --> 00:21:55,705
non-negative.

393
00:21:55,705 --> 00:22:00,585
We will show that probabilities
are also less

394
00:22:00,585 --> 00:22:03,175
than or equal to 1.

395
00:22:03,175 --> 00:22:06,835
We have another axiom that says
that the probability of

396
00:22:06,835 --> 00:22:09,415
the entire sample space is 1.

397
00:22:09,415 --> 00:22:12,315
We will show a counterpart that
the probability of the

398
00:22:12,315 --> 00:22:15,115
empty set is equal to 0.

399
00:22:15,115 --> 00:22:16,495
This makes perfect sense.

400
00:22:16,495 --> 00:22:20,295
The empty set has no elements,
so it is impossible.

401
00:22:20,295 --> 00:22:24,295
There is 0 probability that the
outcome of the experiment

402
00:22:24,295 --> 00:22:27,365
would lie in the empty set.

403
00:22:27,365 --> 00:22:30,005
We also have another
intuitive property.

404
00:22:30,005 --> 00:22:34,035
The probability that an event
happens plus the probability

405
00:22:34,035 --> 00:22:37,125
that the vendor does not
happen exhaust all

406
00:22:37,125 --> 00:22:38,295
possibilities.

407
00:22:38,295 --> 00:22:41,205
And these two probabilities
together should add to 1.

408
00:22:41,205 --> 00:22:44,495
For instance, if the probability
of heads is 0.6,

409
00:22:44,495 --> 00:22:49,005
then the probability of
tails should be 0.4.

410
00:22:49,005 --> 00:22:52,675
Finally, we can generalize the
additivity axiom, which was

411
00:22:52,675 --> 00:22:58,895
originally given for the case of
two disjoint events to the

412
00:22:58,895 --> 00:23:01,845
case where we're dealing
with the union of

413
00:23:01,845 --> 00:23:05,265
several disjoint events.

414
00:23:05,265 --> 00:23:09,715
By disjoint here we mean that
the intersection of any two of

415
00:23:09,715 --> 00:23:12,415
these events is the empty set.

416
00:23:12,415 --> 00:23:15,545
We will prove this for the case
of three events and then

417
00:23:15,545 --> 00:23:18,885
the argument generalizes for
the case where we're taking

418
00:23:18,885 --> 00:23:22,065
the union of k disjoint
events, where k

419
00:23:22,065 --> 00:23:24,325
is any finite number.

420
00:23:24,325 --> 00:23:26,785
So the intuition of this result
is the same as for the

421
00:23:26,785 --> 00:23:28,305
case of two events.

422
00:23:28,305 --> 00:23:32,055
But we will derive it formally
and we will also use it to

423
00:23:32,055 --> 00:23:34,834
come up with a way of
calculating the probability of

424
00:23:34,834 --> 00:23:39,315
a finite set by simply adding
the probabilities of its

425
00:23:39,315 --> 00:23:41,375
individual elements.

426
00:23:41,375 --> 00:23:44,685
All of these statements
that we just

427
00:23:44,685 --> 00:23:47,225
presented are intuitive.

428
00:23:47,225 --> 00:23:49,005
And you do not to really
need to be

429
00:23:49,005 --> 00:23:51,335
convinced about their validity.

430
00:23:51,335 --> 00:23:54,515
Nevertheless, it is instructive
to see how these

431
00:23:54,515 --> 00:23:57,835
statements follow from
the axioms that

432
00:23:57,835 --> 00:23:59,085
we have put in place.

433
00:23:59,085 --> 00:24:01,785


434
00:24:01,785 --> 00:24:06,125
So we will now present the
arguments based only on the

435
00:24:06,125 --> 00:24:08,265
three axioms that we
have available.

436
00:24:08,265 --> 00:24:12,015
And in order to be able to refer
to these axioms, let us

437
00:24:12,015 --> 00:24:17,885
give them some names, call
them axioms A, B, and C.

438
00:24:17,885 --> 00:24:19,505
We start as follows.

439
00:24:19,505 --> 00:24:23,965
Let us look at the sample space
and a subset of that

440
00:24:23,965 --> 00:24:25,215
sample space.

441
00:24:25,215 --> 00:24:30,125
Call it A. And consider the
complement of that subset.

442
00:24:30,125 --> 00:24:33,385
The complement is the set of
all elements that do not

443
00:24:33,385 --> 00:24:39,325
belong to the set A. So a set
together with its complement

444
00:24:39,325 --> 00:24:43,535
make up everything, which is
the entire sample space.

445
00:24:43,535 --> 00:24:46,255
On the other hand, if an element
belongs to a set A, it

446
00:24:46,255 --> 00:24:48,315
does not belong to
its complement.

447
00:24:48,315 --> 00:24:50,865
So the intersection of a
set with its complement

448
00:24:50,865 --> 00:24:53,595
is the empty set.

449
00:24:53,595 --> 00:24:57,785
Now we argue as follows.

450
00:24:57,785 --> 00:25:02,295
We have that the probability of
the entire sample space is

451
00:25:02,295 --> 00:25:05,085
equal to 1.

452
00:25:05,085 --> 00:25:08,875
This is true by our
second axiom.

453
00:25:08,875 --> 00:25:12,215
Now the sample space, as we just
discussed, can be written

454
00:25:12,215 --> 00:25:16,865
as the union of an event and the
complement of that event.

455
00:25:16,865 --> 00:25:21,185
This is just a set theoretic
relation.

456
00:25:21,185 --> 00:25:27,525
And next since a set and its
complement our disjoint, this

457
00:25:27,525 --> 00:25:31,595
means that we can apply the
additivity axiom and write

458
00:25:31,595 --> 00:25:36,135
this probability as the sum of
the probability of event A

459
00:25:36,135 --> 00:25:40,825
with the probability of the
complement of A. This is one

460
00:25:40,825 --> 00:25:44,765
of the relations that we had
claimed and which we have now

461
00:25:44,765 --> 00:25:46,305
established.

462
00:25:46,305 --> 00:25:49,885
Based on this relation, we
can also write that the

463
00:25:49,885 --> 00:25:54,185
probability of an event A
is equal to 1 minus the

464
00:25:54,185 --> 00:25:58,025
probability of the complement
of that event.

465
00:25:58,025 --> 00:26:01,855
And because, by the
non-negativity axiom this

466
00:26:01,855 --> 00:26:06,145
quantity here is non-negative, 1
minus something non-negative

467
00:26:06,145 --> 00:26:08,555
is less than or equal to 1.

468
00:26:08,555 --> 00:26:11,105
We're using here the
non-negativity axiom.

469
00:26:11,105 --> 00:26:14,055
And we have established another
property, namely that

470
00:26:14,055 --> 00:26:20,015
probabilities are always less
than or equal to 1.

471
00:26:20,015 --> 00:26:31,495
Finally, let us note that 1 is
the probability, always, of a

472
00:26:31,495 --> 00:26:37,255
set plus the probability of
a complement of that set.

473
00:26:37,255 --> 00:26:40,385
And let us use this property for
the case where the set of

474
00:26:40,385 --> 00:26:44,595
interest is the entire
sample space.

475
00:26:44,595 --> 00:26:48,355
Now, the probability of the
entire sample space is itself

476
00:26:48,355 --> 00:26:51,315
equal to 1.

477
00:26:51,315 --> 00:26:55,135
And what is the complement of
the entire sample space?

478
00:26:55,135 --> 00:26:58,085
The complement of the entire
sample space consists of all

479
00:26:58,085 --> 00:27:00,625
elements that do not belong
to the sample space.

480
00:27:00,625 --> 00:27:04,865
But since the sample space is
supposed to contain all

481
00:27:04,865 --> 00:27:07,605
possible elements,
its complement is

482
00:27:07,605 --> 00:27:09,995
just the empty set.

483
00:27:09,995 --> 00:27:12,705
And from this relation we get
the implication that the

484
00:27:12,705 --> 00:27:17,395
probability of the empty
set is equal to 0.

485
00:27:17,395 --> 00:27:20,955
This establishes yet one more of
the properties that we had

486
00:27:20,955 --> 00:27:22,665
just claimed a little earlier.

487
00:27:22,665 --> 00:27:26,635


488
00:27:26,635 --> 00:27:29,965
We finally come to the proof of
the generalization of our

489
00:27:29,965 --> 00:27:33,685
additivity axiom from the case
of two disjoint events to the

490
00:27:33,685 --> 00:27:36,115
case of three disjoint events.

491
00:27:36,115 --> 00:27:38,995
So we have our sample space.

492
00:27:38,995 --> 00:27:42,225
And within that sample
space we have three

493
00:27:42,225 --> 00:27:44,485
events, three subsets.

494
00:27:44,485 --> 00:27:47,745
And these subsets are disjoint
in the sense that any two of

495
00:27:47,745 --> 00:27:51,495
those subsets have no
elements in common.

496
00:27:51,495 --> 00:27:55,765
And we're interested in the
probability of the union of A,

497
00:27:55,765 --> 00:27:57,225
B, and C.

498
00:27:57,225 --> 00:27:59,045
How do we make progress?

499
00:27:59,045 --> 00:28:02,325
We have an additivity axiom in
our hands, which applies to

500
00:28:02,325 --> 00:28:05,485
the case of the union of
two disjoint sets.

501
00:28:05,485 --> 00:28:07,225
Here we have three of them.

502
00:28:07,225 --> 00:28:09,445
But we can do the
following trick.

503
00:28:09,445 --> 00:28:13,545
We can think of the union of A,
B, and C as consisting of

504
00:28:13,545 --> 00:28:21,525
the union of this blue set
with that green set.

505
00:28:21,525 --> 00:28:25,295
Formally, what we're doing is
that we're expressing the

506
00:28:25,295 --> 00:28:28,365
union of these three
sets as follows.

507
00:28:28,365 --> 00:28:33,905
We form one set by taking the
union of A with B. And we have

508
00:28:33,905 --> 00:28:37,795
the other set C. And the overall
union can be thought

509
00:28:37,795 --> 00:28:40,655
of as the union of
these two sets.

510
00:28:40,655 --> 00:28:44,435
Now since the three sets are
disjoint, this implies that

511
00:28:44,435 --> 00:28:48,405
the blue set is disjoint from
the green set and so we can

512
00:28:48,405 --> 00:28:52,565
use the additivity axiom here
to write this probability as

513
00:28:52,565 --> 00:28:59,885
the probability of A union B
plus the probability of C. And

514
00:28:59,885 --> 00:29:03,145
now we can use the additivity
axiom once more since the sets

515
00:29:03,145 --> 00:29:07,235
A and B are disjoint to write
the first term as probability

516
00:29:07,235 --> 00:29:11,595
of A plus probability of B. We
carry over the last term and

517
00:29:11,595 --> 00:29:14,775
we have the relation that
we wanted to prove.

518
00:29:14,775 --> 00:29:16,985
This is the proof for the
case of three events.

519
00:29:16,985 --> 00:29:20,635
You should be able to follow
this line of proof to write an

520
00:29:20,635 --> 00:29:23,425
argument for the case of
four events and so on.

521
00:29:23,425 --> 00:29:26,325
And you might want to continue
by induction.

522
00:29:26,325 --> 00:29:30,915
And eventually you should be
able to prove that if the sets

523
00:29:30,915 --> 00:29:42,875
A1 up to Ak are disjoint then
the probability of the union

524
00:29:42,875 --> 00:29:51,865
of those sets is going to be
equal to the sum of their

525
00:29:51,865 --> 00:29:54,725
individual probabilities.

526
00:29:54,725 --> 00:29:57,755
So this is the generalization
to the case where we're

527
00:29:57,755 --> 00:30:04,145
dealing with the union of
finitely many disjoint events.

528
00:30:04,145 --> 00:30:09,715
A very useful application of
this comes in the case where

529
00:30:09,715 --> 00:30:15,375
we want to calculate the
probability of a finite set.

530
00:30:15,375 --> 00:30:19,535
So here we have a
sample space.

531
00:30:19,535 --> 00:30:24,485
And within that sample space
we have some particular

532
00:30:24,485 --> 00:30:28,945
elements S1, S2, up
to Sk, k of them.

533
00:30:28,945 --> 00:30:34,145
And these elements together
form a finite set.

534
00:30:34,145 --> 00:30:36,045
What can we say about
the probability

535
00:30:36,045 --> 00:30:38,425
of this finite set?

536
00:30:38,425 --> 00:30:43,845
The idea is to take this finite
set that consists of k

537
00:30:43,845 --> 00:30:49,375
elements and think of it as the
union of several little

538
00:30:49,375 --> 00:30:54,385
sets that contain one
element each.

539
00:30:54,385 --> 00:30:57,585
So set theoretically what we're
doing is that we're

540
00:30:57,585 --> 00:31:01,555
taking this set with k elements
and we write it as

541
00:31:01,555 --> 00:31:05,785
the union of a set that contains
just S1, a set that

542
00:31:05,785 --> 00:31:10,395
contains just the second element
S2, and so on, up to

543
00:31:10,395 --> 00:31:11,645
the k-th element.

544
00:31:11,645 --> 00:31:14,285


545
00:31:14,285 --> 00:31:17,375
We're assuming, of course, that
these elements are all

546
00:31:17,375 --> 00:31:19,585
different from each other.

547
00:31:19,585 --> 00:31:23,205
So in that case, these sets,
these single element sets, are

548
00:31:23,205 --> 00:31:24,585
all disjoint.

549
00:31:24,585 --> 00:31:28,565
So using the additivity property
for a union of k

550
00:31:28,565 --> 00:31:33,875
disjoint sets, we can write
this as the sum of the

551
00:31:33,875 --> 00:31:37,785
probabilities of the different
single element sets.

552
00:31:37,785 --> 00:31:43,345


553
00:31:43,345 --> 00:31:47,755
At this point, it is usual to
start abusing, or rather,

554
00:31:47,755 --> 00:31:50,145
simplifying notation
a little bit.

555
00:31:50,145 --> 00:31:52,595
Probabilities are assigned
to sets.

556
00:31:52,595 --> 00:31:55,655
So here we're talking about the
probability of a set that

557
00:31:55,655 --> 00:31:57,485
contains a single element.

558
00:31:57,485 --> 00:32:01,445
But intuitively, we can also
talk as just the probability

559
00:32:01,445 --> 00:32:06,455
of that particular element and
use this simpler notation.

560
00:32:06,455 --> 00:32:10,025
So when using the simpler
notation, we will be talking

561
00:32:10,025 --> 00:32:13,505
about the probabilities of
individual elements.

562
00:32:13,505 --> 00:32:16,535
Although in terms of formal
mathematics, what we really

563
00:32:16,535 --> 00:32:23,065
mean is the probability of this
event that's comprised

564
00:32:23,065 --> 00:32:27,455
only of a particular element
S1 and so on.

565
00:32:27,455 --> 00:32:30,675


566
00:32:30,738 --> 00:32:32,848


567
00:32:32,848 --> 00:32:35,568
We will now continue and
derive some additional

568
00:32:35,568 --> 00:32:39,298
properties of probability laws
which are, again, consequences

569
00:32:39,298 --> 00:32:42,418
of the axioms that we
have introduced.

570
00:32:42,418 --> 00:32:44,878
The first property
is the following.

571
00:32:44,878 --> 00:32:48,228
If we have two sets
and one set is

572
00:32:48,228 --> 00:32:50,908
smaller than the other--

573
00:32:50,908 --> 00:32:53,008
so we have a picture
as follows.

574
00:32:53,008 --> 00:32:54,858
We have our sample space.

575
00:32:54,858 --> 00:32:57,818
And we have a certain set, A.

576
00:32:57,818 --> 00:33:03,518
And then we have a certain set,
B, which is even bigger.

577
00:33:03,518 --> 00:33:08,968
So the set B is the
bigger blue set.

578
00:33:08,968 --> 00:33:15,628
So if B is a set which is
larger than A, then,

579
00:33:15,628 --> 00:33:18,938
naturally, the probability that
the outcome falls inside

580
00:33:18,938 --> 00:33:22,028
B should be at least as big as
the probability that the

581
00:33:22,028 --> 00:33:24,118
outcome falls inside A.

582
00:33:24,118 --> 00:33:26,858
How do we prove this formally?

583
00:33:26,858 --> 00:33:33,148
The set B can be expressed
as a union of two pieces.

584
00:33:33,148 --> 00:33:37,028
One piece is the set A itself.

585
00:33:37,028 --> 00:33:44,038
The second piece is whatever
elements of B there are, that

586
00:33:44,038 --> 00:33:48,768
do not belong in A. What
are these elements?

587
00:33:48,768 --> 00:33:54,528
They are elements that belong
to B. And they do not belong

588
00:33:54,528 --> 00:33:58,458
to A, which means that they
belong to the complement of A.

589
00:33:58,458 --> 00:34:02,788
So we have expressed the set B
as the union of two pieces.

590
00:34:02,788 --> 00:34:07,668
Now this piece is A. This piece
here is outside A. So

591
00:34:07,668 --> 00:34:09,718
these two pieces are disjoint.

592
00:34:09,718 --> 00:34:12,928
And so we can apply the
additivity axiom, and write

593
00:34:12,928 --> 00:34:18,208
that the probability of B is
equal to the probability of A

594
00:34:18,208 --> 00:34:21,338
plus the probability
of the other set.

595
00:34:21,338 --> 00:34:24,308


596
00:34:24,308 --> 00:34:28,698
And since probabilities are
non-negative, this expression

597
00:34:28,698 --> 00:34:33,598
here is at least as large as the
probability of A. And this

598
00:34:33,598 --> 00:34:35,448
concludes the proof of
the property that

599
00:34:35,448 --> 00:34:36,378
we wanted to show.

600
00:34:36,378 --> 00:34:39,717
Indeed, the probability of A is
less than or equal to the

601
00:34:39,717 --> 00:34:43,508
probability of B.

602
00:34:43,508 --> 00:34:46,938
The next property we will
show is the following.

603
00:34:46,938 --> 00:34:50,698
It allows us to write the
probability of the union of

604
00:34:50,698 --> 00:34:54,778
two sets for the case now, where
the two sets are not

605
00:34:54,778 --> 00:34:57,258
necessarily disjoint.

606
00:34:57,258 --> 00:35:02,198
So the picture is as follows.

607
00:35:02,198 --> 00:35:11,178
We have our two sets, A
and B. These sets are

608
00:35:11,178 --> 00:35:14,108
not necessarily disjoint.

609
00:35:14,108 --> 00:35:16,688
And we want to say something
about the probability of the

610
00:35:16,688 --> 00:35:21,618
union of A and B.

611
00:35:21,618 --> 00:35:25,508
Now the union of A and B
consists of three pieces.

612
00:35:25,508 --> 00:35:30,178
One piece is this one here.

613
00:35:30,178 --> 00:35:36,838
And that piece consists of those
elements of A that do

614
00:35:36,838 --> 00:35:42,818
not belong to B. So they
belong to B complement.

615
00:35:42,818 --> 00:35:46,078
This set has a certain
probability, let's call it

616
00:35:46,078 --> 00:35:50,578
little a and indicate
it on this diagram.

617
00:35:50,578 --> 00:35:54,148
So a is the probability
of this piece.

618
00:35:54,148 --> 00:35:57,038
Another piece is this one
here, which is the

619
00:35:57,038 --> 00:36:01,068
intersection of A and B. It has
a certain probability that

620
00:36:01,068 --> 00:36:03,108
we denote by little b.

621
00:36:03,108 --> 00:36:07,248
This is the probability
of A intersection B.

622
00:36:07,248 --> 00:36:11,468
And finally, there's another
piece, which is out here.

623
00:36:11,468 --> 00:36:14,688
And that piece has a certain
probability c.

624
00:36:14,688 --> 00:36:17,018
It is the probability
of that set.

625
00:36:17,018 --> 00:36:18,748
And what is that set?

626
00:36:18,748 --> 00:36:20,768
That set is the following.

627
00:36:20,768 --> 00:36:24,588
It's that part of B that
consists of elements that do

628
00:36:24,588 --> 00:36:29,258
not belong in A. So it's
B intersection with the

629
00:36:29,258 --> 00:36:33,208
complement of A.

630
00:36:33,208 --> 00:36:36,988
Now let's express the two sides
of this equality here in

631
00:36:36,988 --> 00:36:40,018
terms of little a, little b, and
little c, and see whether

632
00:36:40,018 --> 00:36:41,928
we get the same thing.

633
00:36:41,928 --> 00:36:48,538
So the probability of A union B.
A union B consists of these

634
00:36:48,538 --> 00:36:52,738
three pieces that have
probabilities little a, little

635
00:36:52,738 --> 00:36:55,548
b, and little c, respectively.

636
00:36:55,548 --> 00:36:58,448
And by the additivity axiom, the
probability of the union

637
00:36:58,448 --> 00:37:00,738
of A and B is the sum
of the probabilities

638
00:37:00,738 --> 00:37:02,938
of these three pieces.

639
00:37:02,938 --> 00:37:07,218
Let's look now at the right hand
side of that equation and

640
00:37:07,218 --> 00:37:09,538
see whether we get
the same thing.

641
00:37:09,538 --> 00:37:13,808
The probability of A plus the
probability of B, minus the

642
00:37:13,808 --> 00:37:19,888
probability of A intersection
B is equal to the following.

643
00:37:19,888 --> 00:37:23,218
A consists of two pieces that
have probabilities little a

644
00:37:23,218 --> 00:37:26,278
and little b.

645
00:37:26,278 --> 00:37:28,978
The set B consists of two pieces
that have probabilities

646
00:37:28,978 --> 00:37:30,503
little b and little c.

647
00:37:30,503 --> 00:37:33,368


648
00:37:33,368 --> 00:37:35,538
And then we subtract the
probability of the

649
00:37:35,538 --> 00:37:38,698
intersection, which is b.

650
00:37:38,698 --> 00:37:44,478
And we notice that we can
cancel here one b

651
00:37:44,478 --> 00:37:45,948
with another b.

652
00:37:45,948 --> 00:37:54,028
And what we are left with
is a plus b plus c.

653
00:37:54,028 --> 00:37:55,948
So this checks.

654
00:37:55,948 --> 00:38:01,208
And indeed we have this
equality here.

655
00:38:01,208 --> 00:38:04,068
We have verified that
it is true.

656
00:38:04,068 --> 00:38:08,458
One particular consequence of
the equality that we derived

657
00:38:08,458 --> 00:38:09,878
is the following.

658
00:38:09,878 --> 00:38:19,148
Since this term here is always
non-negative, this means that

659
00:38:19,148 --> 00:38:24,128
the probability of A union B is
always less than or equal

660
00:38:24,128 --> 00:38:28,168
to the probability of A plus
the probability of B. This

661
00:38:28,168 --> 00:38:31,498
inequality here is quite useful
whenever we want to

662
00:38:31,498 --> 00:38:33,348
argue that a certain
probability is

663
00:38:33,348 --> 00:38:34,588
smaller than something.

664
00:38:34,588 --> 00:38:36,198
And it has a name.

665
00:38:36,198 --> 00:38:37,993
It's called the union bound.

666
00:38:37,993 --> 00:38:43,758


667
00:38:43,758 --> 00:38:48,388
We finally consider one last
consequence of our axioms.

668
00:38:48,388 --> 00:38:52,268
And namely, we are going to
derive an expression, a way of

669
00:38:52,268 --> 00:38:57,208
calculating the probability of
the union of three sets, not

670
00:38:57,208 --> 00:38:59,568
necessarily disjoint.

671
00:38:59,568 --> 00:39:02,628
So we have our sample space.

672
00:39:02,628 --> 00:39:08,390
And within the sample space
there are three sets-- set A,

673
00:39:08,390 --> 00:39:17,368
set B, and set C.

674
00:39:17,368 --> 00:39:20,908
We are going to use a set
theoretic relation.

675
00:39:20,908 --> 00:39:26,578
We are going to express the
union of these three sets as

676
00:39:26,578 --> 00:39:31,108
the union of three
disjoint pieces.

677
00:39:31,108 --> 00:39:34,638
What are these disjoint
pieces?

678
00:39:34,638 --> 00:39:37,861
One piece is the set A itself.

679
00:39:37,861 --> 00:39:43,788


680
00:39:43,788 --> 00:39:50,238
The second piece is going to
be that part of B which is

681
00:39:50,238 --> 00:39:57,478
outside A. So this is the
intersection of B with the

682
00:39:57,478 --> 00:40:01,598
complement of A.

683
00:40:01,598 --> 00:40:09,328
The third piece is going to be
whatever is left in order to

684
00:40:09,328 --> 00:40:11,138
form the union of
the three sets.

685
00:40:11,138 --> 00:40:16,718
What is left is that part of C
that does not belong to A and

686
00:40:16,718 --> 00:40:23,758
that does not belong to B. So
that part is C intersection

687
00:40:23,758 --> 00:40:28,908
with A complement and
B complement.

688
00:40:28,908 --> 00:40:33,118
Now this set here, of course,
is the same as that set

689
00:40:33,118 --> 00:40:35,968
because intersection of two sets
is the same no matter in

690
00:40:35,968 --> 00:40:38,168
which order we take
the two sets.

691
00:40:38,168 --> 00:40:42,148
And similarly, the set that we
have here is the same one that

692
00:40:42,148 --> 00:40:44,638
appears in that expression.

693
00:40:44,638 --> 00:40:47,578
Now we notice that these three
pieces, the red, the blue, and

694
00:40:47,578 --> 00:40:50,298
the green, are disjoint
from each other.

695
00:40:50,298 --> 00:40:56,558
So by the additivity axiom, the
probability of this union

696
00:40:56,558 --> 00:41:01,818
here is going to be the sum
of the probabilities

697
00:41:01,818 --> 00:41:03,938
of the three pieces.

698
00:41:03,938 --> 00:41:06,208
And that's exactly
the expression

699
00:41:06,208 --> 00:41:07,458
the we have up here.

700
00:41:07,458 --> 00:41:10,104


701
00:41:10,168 --> 00:41:10,928


702
00:41:10,928 --> 00:41:15,108
Let us now move from the
abstract to the concrete.

703
00:41:15,108 --> 00:41:17,758
Recall the example that we
discussed earlier where we

704
00:41:17,758 --> 00:41:20,368
have two rolls of a
tetrahedral die.

705
00:41:20,368 --> 00:41:22,548
So there are 16 possible
outcomes

706
00:41:22,548 --> 00:41:25,238
illustrated in this diagram.

707
00:41:25,238 --> 00:41:29,538
To continue, now we need to
specify a probability law,

708
00:41:29,538 --> 00:41:32,238
some kind of probability
assignment.

709
00:41:32,238 --> 00:41:35,788
To keep things simple, we're
going to make the assumption

710
00:41:35,788 --> 00:41:40,558
that the 16 possible outcomes
are all equally likely.

711
00:41:40,558 --> 00:41:45,738
And each outcome has a
probability of 1 over 16.

712
00:41:45,738 --> 00:41:49,038
Given this assumption, we will
now proceed to calculate

713
00:41:49,038 --> 00:41:51,498
certain probabilities.

714
00:41:51,498 --> 00:41:56,358
Let us look first at the
probability that X, which

715
00:41:56,358 --> 00:42:00,958
stands the result of the first
roll, is equal to 1.

716
00:42:00,958 --> 00:42:04,758
The way to calculate this
probability is to identify

717
00:42:04,758 --> 00:42:09,498
what exactly that event is in
our picture of the sample

718
00:42:09,498 --> 00:42:12,298
space, and then calculate.

719
00:42:12,298 --> 00:42:16,768
The event that X is equal to 1
can happen in four different

720
00:42:16,768 --> 00:42:23,098
ways that correspond to these
four particular outcomes.

721
00:42:23,098 --> 00:42:27,498
Each one of these outcomes has
a probability of 1 over 16.

722
00:42:27,498 --> 00:42:31,638
The probability of this event
is the sum of the

723
00:42:31,638 --> 00:42:34,298
probabilities of the outcomes
that it contains.

724
00:42:34,298 --> 00:42:42,908
So it is 4 times 1 over 16,
equal to one fourth.

725
00:42:42,908 --> 00:42:47,948
Let now Z stand for the smaller
of the two numbers

726
00:42:47,948 --> 00:42:50,718
that came up in our two rolls.

727
00:42:50,718 --> 00:42:59,628
So for example, if X is 2 and
Y is equal to 3, then Z is

728
00:42:59,628 --> 00:43:04,028
equal to 2, which is the
smaller of the two.

729
00:43:04,028 --> 00:43:07,568
Let us try to calculate the
probability that the smaller

730
00:43:07,568 --> 00:43:11,218
of the two outcomes
is equal to 4.

731
00:43:11,218 --> 00:43:15,548
Now for the smaller of the two
outcomes to be equal to 4, we

732
00:43:15,548 --> 00:43:20,538
must have that both X and
Y are equal to 4.

733
00:43:20,538 --> 00:43:25,168
So this outcome here is the only
way that this particular

734
00:43:25,168 --> 00:43:27,198
event can happen.

735
00:43:27,198 --> 00:43:31,108
Since there's only one outcome
that makes the event happen,

736
00:43:31,108 --> 00:43:33,928
the probability of this event
is the probability of that

737
00:43:33,928 --> 00:43:36,863
outcome and is equal
to 1 over 16.

738
00:43:36,863 --> 00:43:39,478


739
00:43:39,478 --> 00:43:42,928
For another example, let's
calculate the probability that

740
00:43:42,928 --> 00:43:45,388
the minimum is equal to 2.

741
00:43:45,388 --> 00:43:47,838
What does it mean that the
minimum is equal to 2?

742
00:43:47,838 --> 00:43:52,658
It means that one of the dice
resulted in a 2, and the other

743
00:43:52,658 --> 00:43:56,268
die resulted in a number
that's 2 or larger.

744
00:43:56,268 --> 00:43:59,018
So we could have both
equal to 2.

745
00:43:59,018 --> 00:44:03,508
We could have X equal
to 2, but Y larger.

746
00:44:03,508 --> 00:44:09,268
Or we could have Y equal to
2 and X something larger.

747
00:44:09,268 --> 00:44:13,528
This green event, this green
set, is the set of all

748
00:44:13,528 --> 00:44:16,938
outcomes for which the
minimum of the two

749
00:44:16,938 --> 00:44:18,468
rolls is equal to 2.

750
00:44:18,468 --> 00:44:21,228
There's a total of five
such outcomes.

751
00:44:21,228 --> 00:44:24,778
Each one of them has
probably 1 over 16.

752
00:44:24,778 --> 00:44:28,648
And we have discussed that for
finite sets, the probability

753
00:44:28,648 --> 00:44:32,538
of a finite set is the sum of
the probabilities of the

754
00:44:32,538 --> 00:44:33,948
elements of that set.

755
00:44:33,948 --> 00:44:36,888
So we have five elements
here, each one with

756
00:44:36,888 --> 00:44:38,928
probability 1 over 16.

757
00:44:38,928 --> 00:44:43,378
And this is the answer
to this problem.

758
00:44:43,378 --> 00:44:49,298
This particular example that we
saw here is a special case

759
00:44:49,298 --> 00:44:53,088
of what is called a discrete
uniform law.

760
00:44:53,088 --> 00:44:55,938
In a discrete uniform law,
we have a sample

761
00:44:55,938 --> 00:44:58,093
space which is finite.

762
00:44:58,093 --> 00:45:00,798


763
00:45:00,798 --> 00:45:03,428
And it has n elements.

764
00:45:03,428 --> 00:45:08,198
And we assume that these n
elements are equally likely.

765
00:45:08,198 --> 00:45:11,268
Now since the probability of
omega, the probability of the

766
00:45:11,268 --> 00:45:16,338
entire sample space, is equal to
1, this means that each one

767
00:45:16,338 --> 00:45:21,148
of these elements must have
probability 1 over n.

768
00:45:21,148 --> 00:45:24,388
That's the only way that the
sum of the probabilities of

769
00:45:24,388 --> 00:45:28,778
the different outcomes would be
equal to 1 as required by

770
00:45:28,778 --> 00:45:31,497
the normalization axiom.

771
00:45:31,497 --> 00:45:35,798
Consider now some subset of the
sample space, an event A

772
00:45:35,798 --> 00:45:38,008
that, exactly k elements.

773
00:45:38,008 --> 00:45:41,488
What is the probability
of the set A?

774
00:45:41,488 --> 00:45:45,148
It's the sum of the
probabilities of its elements.

775
00:45:45,148 --> 00:45:47,508
There are k elements.

776
00:45:47,508 --> 00:45:51,928
And each one of them has a
probability of 1 over n.

777
00:45:51,928 --> 00:45:55,738
And this way we can find the
probability of the set A.

778
00:45:55,738 --> 00:46:01,238
So when we have a discrete
uniform probability law, we

779
00:46:01,238 --> 00:46:05,278
can calculate probabilities by
simply counting the number of

780
00:46:05,278 --> 00:46:09,288
elements of omega, which is n,
finding the number n, and

781
00:46:09,288 --> 00:46:12,678
counting the number of elements
of the set A. That's

782
00:46:12,678 --> 00:46:14,908
the reason why counting
will turn out to be

783
00:46:14,908 --> 00:46:16,278
an important skill.

784
00:46:16,278 --> 00:46:19,908
And there will be a whole
lecture devoted to this

785
00:46:19,908 --> 00:46:21,158
particular topic.

786
00:46:21,158 --> 00:46:23,001


787
00:46:23,057 --> 00:46:23,227


788
00:46:23,227 --> 00:46:26,457
We will now go through a
probability calculation for

789
00:46:26,457 --> 00:46:29,807
the case where we have a
continuous sample space.

790
00:46:29,807 --> 00:46:33,877
We revisit our earlier example
in which we were throwing a

791
00:46:33,877 --> 00:46:37,897
dart into a square target,
the square target

792
00:46:37,897 --> 00:46:39,067
being the unit square.

793
00:46:39,067 --> 00:46:42,027
And we were guaranteed that our
dart would fall somewhere

794
00:46:42,027 --> 00:46:43,837
inside this set.

795
00:46:43,837 --> 00:46:47,147
So our sample space is the
unit square itself.

796
00:46:47,147 --> 00:46:50,727
We have a description of the
sample space, but we do not

797
00:46:50,727 --> 00:46:52,197
yet have a probability law.

798
00:46:52,197 --> 00:46:54,397
We need to specify one.

799
00:46:54,397 --> 00:46:58,757
The choice of a probability
law could be arbitrary.

800
00:46:58,757 --> 00:47:02,677
It's up to us to choose how to
model a certain situation.

801
00:47:02,677 --> 00:47:05,837
And to keep things simple, we're
going to assume that our

802
00:47:05,837 --> 00:47:11,087
probability law is a uniform
one, which means that the

803
00:47:11,087 --> 00:47:14,407
probability of any particular
subset of the sample space is

804
00:47:14,407 --> 00:47:17,097
going to be the area
of that subset.

805
00:47:17,097 --> 00:47:20,827
So if we have some subset lying
somewhere here and we

806
00:47:20,827 --> 00:47:24,247
ask what is the probability that
we fall into that subset?

807
00:47:24,247 --> 00:47:27,527
The probability is exactly
the area of

808
00:47:27,527 --> 00:47:30,067
that particular subset.

809
00:47:30,067 --> 00:47:32,917
Once more, this is an arbitrary
choice of a

810
00:47:32,917 --> 00:47:34,057
probability law.

811
00:47:34,057 --> 00:47:37,197
There's nothing in our
assumptions so far that would

812
00:47:37,197 --> 00:47:39,427
force us to make this
particular choice.

813
00:47:39,427 --> 00:47:43,357
And we just use it for the
purposes of this example.

814
00:47:43,357 --> 00:47:47,427
So now let us calculate
some probabilities.

815
00:47:47,427 --> 00:47:49,807
Let us look at this event.

816
00:47:49,807 --> 00:47:55,597
This is the event that the sum
of the two numbers that we get

817
00:47:55,597 --> 00:47:59,497
in our experiment is less
than or equal to 1/2.

818
00:47:59,497 --> 00:48:03,147
It is always useful to work in
terms of a picture and to

819
00:48:03,147 --> 00:48:07,317
depict that event in a picture
of the sample space.

820
00:48:07,317 --> 00:48:11,697
So in terms of that sample
space, the points that make

821
00:48:11,697 --> 00:48:18,957
this event to be true are just a
triangle that lies below the

822
00:48:18,957 --> 00:48:25,997
line, where this is the line,
that's x plus y equals 1/2.

823
00:48:25,997 --> 00:48:29,917
Anything below that line, these
are the outcomes that

824
00:48:29,917 --> 00:48:32,036
make this event happen.

825
00:48:32,036 --> 00:48:35,417
So we're trying to find the
probability of this red event.

826
00:48:35,417 --> 00:48:39,097
We have assumed that probability
is equal to area.

827
00:48:39,097 --> 00:48:42,367
Therefore, the probability we're
trying to calculate is

828
00:48:42,367 --> 00:48:44,237
the area of a triangle.

829
00:48:44,237 --> 00:48:48,937
And the area of a triangle is
1/2 times the base of the

830
00:48:48,937 --> 00:48:53,277
triangle, which is 1/2 in our
case, times the height of the

831
00:48:53,277 --> 00:48:56,707
triangle, which is again
1/2 in our case.

832
00:48:56,707 --> 00:48:58,877
And the end result is 1/8.

833
00:48:58,877 --> 00:49:01,907


834
00:49:01,907 --> 00:49:06,447
Let us now calculate another
probability.

835
00:49:06,447 --> 00:49:09,787
Now, this is an event
that consists of

836
00:49:09,787 --> 00:49:12,297
only a single element.

837
00:49:12,297 --> 00:49:18,817
We take the point 0.5, 0.3,
which sits somewhere here.

838
00:49:18,817 --> 00:49:23,277
The event of interest is a set,
but that set consists of

839
00:49:23,277 --> 00:49:24,437
a single point.

840
00:49:24,437 --> 00:49:27,317
So we're asking for the
probability that our dart

841
00:49:27,317 --> 00:49:31,527
falls exactly on top
of that point.

842
00:49:31,527 --> 00:49:32,267
What is it?

843
00:49:32,267 --> 00:49:35,317
Well, it is the area
of a set that

844
00:49:35,317 --> 00:49:36,807
consists of a single point.

845
00:49:36,807 --> 00:49:39,107
What is the area of
a single point?

846
00:49:39,107 --> 00:49:40,967
It is 0.

847
00:49:40,967 --> 00:49:44,047
And similarly for any other
single point inside that

848
00:49:44,047 --> 00:49:47,147
sample space that we might have
considered, the answer is

849
00:49:47,147 --> 00:49:50,557
going to be 0.

850
00:49:50,557 --> 00:49:54,487
Let us now abstract from this
example, as well as the

851
00:49:54,487 --> 00:49:57,907
previous one, and note
the following.

852
00:49:57,907 --> 00:50:00,267
Probability calculations
involve a

853
00:50:00,267 --> 00:50:03,107
sequence of four steps.

854
00:50:03,107 --> 00:50:07,057
Starting with a word description
of a problem, of a

855
00:50:07,057 --> 00:50:09,727
probabilistic experiment,
we first write

856
00:50:09,727 --> 00:50:12,257
down the sample space.

857
00:50:12,257 --> 00:50:15,547
Then we specify a
probability law.

858
00:50:15,547 --> 00:50:18,327
Let me emphasize again here
that this step has some

859
00:50:18,327 --> 00:50:20,087
arbitrariness in it.

860
00:50:20,087 --> 00:50:22,897
You can choose any probability
law you like, although for

861
00:50:22,897 --> 00:50:25,537
your results to be useful
it would be good if your

862
00:50:25,537 --> 00:50:28,407
probability law captures the
real-world phenomenon you're

863
00:50:28,407 --> 00:50:30,147
trying to model.

864
00:50:30,147 --> 00:50:34,067
Typically you're interested in
calculating the probability of

865
00:50:34,067 --> 00:50:36,227
some event.

866
00:50:36,227 --> 00:50:39,576
That event may be described in
some loose manner, so you need

867
00:50:39,576 --> 00:50:41,777
to describe it mathematically.

868
00:50:41,777 --> 00:50:45,627
And if possible, it's always
good to describe it in terms

869
00:50:45,627 --> 00:50:46,747
of a picture.

870
00:50:46,747 --> 00:50:49,357
Pictures are immensely
useful when going

871
00:50:49,357 --> 00:50:51,407
through this process.

872
00:50:51,407 --> 00:50:56,027
And finally, the last step is to
go ahead and calculate the

873
00:50:56,027 --> 00:50:58,947
probability of the event
of interest.

874
00:50:58,947 --> 00:51:01,747
Now, a probability law in
principle specifies the

875
00:51:01,747 --> 00:51:03,987
probability of every
event, and there's

876
00:51:03,987 --> 00:51:05,917
nothing else to do.

877
00:51:05,917 --> 00:51:08,727
But quite often the probability
law will be given

878
00:51:08,727 --> 00:51:12,597
in some implicit manner, for
example, by specifying the

879
00:51:12,597 --> 00:51:15,447
probabilities of only
some of the events.

880
00:51:15,447 --> 00:51:18,367
In that case, you may have to
do some additional work to

881
00:51:18,367 --> 00:51:21,067
find the probability of
the particular event

882
00:51:21,067 --> 00:51:22,927
that you care about.

883
00:51:22,927 --> 00:51:26,897
This last step sometimes
will be easy.

884
00:51:26,897 --> 00:51:29,397
Sometimes it may
be complicated.

885
00:51:29,397 --> 00:51:33,877
But in either case, by following
this four-step

886
00:51:33,877 --> 00:51:37,847
procedure and by being
systematic you will always be

887
00:51:37,847 --> 00:51:40,587
able to come up with a single
correct answer.

888
00:51:40,587 --> 00:51:42,890


889
00:51:42,944 --> 00:51:43,624


890
00:51:43,624 --> 00:51:46,864
We have seen so far an example
of a probability law on a

891
00:51:46,864 --> 00:51:50,534
discrete and finite sample space
as well as an example

892
00:51:50,534 --> 00:51:53,494
with an infinite and continuous
sample space.

893
00:51:53,494 --> 00:51:57,774
Let us now look at an example
involving a discrete but

894
00:51:57,774 --> 00:52:00,294
infinite sample space.

895
00:52:00,294 --> 00:52:03,754
We carry out an experiment whose
outcome is an arbitrary

896
00:52:03,754 --> 00:52:05,834
positive integer.

897
00:52:05,834 --> 00:52:08,244
As an example of such an
experiment, suppose that we

898
00:52:08,244 --> 00:52:11,554
keep tossing a coin and the
outcome is the number of

899
00:52:11,554 --> 00:52:15,794
tosses until we observe heads
for the first time.

900
00:52:15,794 --> 00:52:19,084
The first heads might appear
in the first toss or the

901
00:52:19,084 --> 00:52:22,094
second or the third,
and so on.

902
00:52:22,094 --> 00:52:25,834
So in this example, any positive
integer is possible.

903
00:52:25,834 --> 00:52:29,264
And so our sample space
is infinite.

904
00:52:29,264 --> 00:52:32,424
Let us not specify a
probability law.

905
00:52:32,424 --> 00:52:35,674
A probability law should
determine the probability of

906
00:52:35,674 --> 00:52:39,894
every event, of every subset
of the sample space.

907
00:52:39,894 --> 00:52:42,024
That is, the probability
of every

908
00:52:42,024 --> 00:52:44,914
set of positive integers.

909
00:52:44,914 --> 00:52:49,084
But instead I will just tell you
the probability of events

910
00:52:49,084 --> 00:52:51,804
that contain a single element.

911
00:52:51,804 --> 00:52:55,994
I'm going to tell you that there
is probability 1 over 2

912
00:52:55,994 --> 00:53:00,954
to the n that the outcome
is equal to n.

913
00:53:00,954 --> 00:53:02,804
Is this good enough?

914
00:53:02,804 --> 00:53:06,744
Is this information enough to
determine the probability of

915
00:53:06,744 --> 00:53:09,364
any subset?

916
00:53:09,364 --> 00:53:11,894
Before we look into that
question, let us first do a

917
00:53:11,894 --> 00:53:15,369
quick sanity check to see
whether these numbers that we

918
00:53:15,369 --> 00:53:18,364
are given look like legitimate
probabilities.

919
00:53:18,364 --> 00:53:20,354
Do they add to 1?

920
00:53:20,354 --> 00:53:22,354
Let's do a quick check.

921
00:53:22,354 --> 00:53:28,784
So the sum over all the possible
values of n of the

922
00:53:28,784 --> 00:53:32,554
probabilities that we're given,
which is an infinite

923
00:53:32,554 --> 00:53:38,464
sum starting from 1, all the way
up to infinity, of 1 over

924
00:53:38,464 --> 00:53:41,644
2 to the n, is equal
to the following.

925
00:53:41,644 --> 00:53:47,194
First we take out a factor of
1/2 from all of these terms,

926
00:53:47,194 --> 00:53:51,024
which reduces the exponent
from n to n minus 1.

927
00:53:51,024 --> 00:53:56,644
This is the same as running
the sum from n equals 0 to

928
00:53:56,644 --> 00:54:02,254
infinity of 1/2 and to the n.

929
00:54:02,254 --> 00:54:07,924
And now we have a usual infinite
geometric series and

930
00:54:07,924 --> 00:54:10,674
we have a formula for this.

931
00:54:10,674 --> 00:54:16,264
The geometric series has a value
of 1 over 1 minus the

932
00:54:16,264 --> 00:54:19,609
number whose power we're
taking, which is 1/2.

933
00:54:19,609 --> 00:54:22,224


934
00:54:22,224 --> 00:54:25,464
And after we do the arithmetic,
this turns out to

935
00:54:25,464 --> 00:54:27,184
be equal to 1.

936
00:54:27,184 --> 00:54:33,804
So indeed, it appears that we
have the basic elements of

937
00:54:33,804 --> 00:54:37,304
what it would take to have a
legitimate probability law.

938
00:54:37,304 --> 00:54:40,814
But now let us look into how
we might calculate the

939
00:54:40,814 --> 00:54:43,454
probability of some
general event.

940
00:54:43,454 --> 00:54:48,314
For example, the probability
that the outcome is even.

941
00:54:48,314 --> 00:54:51,244
We proceed as follows.

942
00:54:51,244 --> 00:54:54,144
The probability that the outcome
is even, this is the

943
00:54:54,144 --> 00:54:58,784
probability of an infinite
set that consists of

944
00:54:58,784 --> 00:55:01,554
all the even integers.

945
00:55:01,554 --> 00:55:05,224


946
00:55:05,224 --> 00:55:12,704
We can write this set as the
union of lots of little sets

947
00:55:12,704 --> 00:55:16,034
that contain a single
element each.

948
00:55:16,034 --> 00:55:19,474
So it's the set containing the
number 2, the set containing

949
00:55:19,474 --> 00:55:21,694
the number 4, the set
containing the

950
00:55:21,694 --> 00:55:24,064
number 6, and so on.

951
00:55:24,064 --> 00:55:26,954


952
00:55:26,954 --> 00:55:30,114
At this point we notice that
we're talking about the

953
00:55:30,114 --> 00:55:34,374
probability of a union of sets
and these sets are disjoint

954
00:55:34,374 --> 00:55:37,704
because they contain
different elements.

955
00:55:37,704 --> 00:55:43,844
So we can use an additivity
property and say that this is

956
00:55:43,844 --> 00:55:48,224
the probability of obtaining a
2, plus the probability of

957
00:55:48,224 --> 00:55:51,134
obtaining a 4, plus
the probability of

958
00:55:51,134 --> 00:55:55,334
obtaining a 6 and so on.

959
00:55:55,334 --> 00:55:58,514
If you're curious about doing
this calculation and actually

960
00:55:58,514 --> 00:56:02,283
obtaining a numerical answer,
you would proceed as follows.

961
00:56:02,283 --> 00:56:08,974
You notice that this is 1 over
2 to the second power plus 1

962
00:56:08,974 --> 00:56:14,314
over 2 to the fourth power plus
1 over 2 to the sixth

963
00:56:14,314 --> 00:56:17,114
power and so on.

964
00:56:17,114 --> 00:56:26,204
Now you factor out a factor of
1/4 and what you're left is 1

965
00:56:26,204 --> 00:56:31,344
plus 1 over 2 to the second
power, which is 1/4, plus 1

966
00:56:31,344 --> 00:56:38,944
over 2 to the fourth power,
which is the same as 1/4 to

967
00:56:38,944 --> 00:56:42,704
the second power and so on.

968
00:56:42,704 --> 00:56:48,384
And now we have 1/4 times the
infinite sum of a geometric

969
00:56:48,384 --> 00:56:55,564
series, which gives us
1 over 1 minus 1/4.

970
00:56:55,564 --> 00:56:59,184
And after you do the algebra you
obtain a numerical answer,

971
00:56:59,184 --> 00:57:00,694
which is equal to 1/3.

972
00:57:00,694 --> 00:57:03,204


973
00:57:03,204 --> 00:57:06,494
But leaving the details of the
calculation aside, the more

974
00:57:06,494 --> 00:57:09,754
important question I want to
address is the following.

975
00:57:09,754 --> 00:57:12,374
Is this calculation correct?

976
00:57:12,374 --> 00:57:15,314
We seem to have used
an additivity

977
00:57:15,314 --> 00:57:17,314
property at this point.

978
00:57:17,314 --> 00:57:20,664


979
00:57:20,664 --> 00:57:24,444
But the additivity properties
that we have in our hands at

980
00:57:24,444 --> 00:57:29,744
this point only talk about
disjoint unions of finitely

981
00:57:29,744 --> 00:57:31,234
many subsets.

982
00:57:31,234 --> 00:57:34,404
Our initial axiom talked about
a disjoint union of two

983
00:57:34,404 --> 00:57:37,934
subsets and then later on we
established a similar property

984
00:57:37,934 --> 00:57:41,764
for a disjoint union of
finitely many subsets.

985
00:57:41,764 --> 00:57:45,564
But here we're talking
about the union of

986
00:57:45,564 --> 00:57:48,714
infinitely many subsets.

987
00:57:48,714 --> 00:57:54,884
So this step here is not really
allowed by what we have

988
00:57:54,884 --> 00:57:56,084
in our hands.

989
00:57:56,084 --> 00:57:59,444
On the other hand, we would like
our theory to allow this

990
00:57:59,444 --> 00:58:01,484
kind of calculation.

991
00:58:01,484 --> 00:58:06,014
The way out of this dilemma is
to introduce an additional

992
00:58:06,014 --> 00:58:09,959
axiom that will indeed allow
this kind of calculation.

993
00:58:09,959 --> 00:58:12,604


994
00:58:12,604 --> 00:58:15,780
The axiom that we introduce
is the following.

995
00:58:15,780 --> 00:58:22,644
If we have an infinite sequence
of disjoint events,

996
00:58:22,644 --> 00:58:25,374
as for example in
this picture.

997
00:58:25,374 --> 00:58:27,504
We have our sample space.

998
00:58:27,504 --> 00:58:29,854
We have a first event, A1.

999
00:58:29,854 --> 00:58:32,384
We have a second event, A2.

1000
00:58:32,384 --> 00:58:34,634
The third event, A3.

1001
00:58:34,634 --> 00:58:38,674
And so we keep continuing and
we have an infinite sequence

1002
00:58:38,674 --> 00:58:40,344
of such events.

1003
00:58:40,344 --> 00:58:45,714
Then the probability of the
union of these events, of

1004
00:58:45,714 --> 00:58:50,544
these infinitely many events, is
the sum of their individual

1005
00:58:50,544 --> 00:58:52,334
probabilities.

1006
00:58:52,334 --> 00:58:58,574
The key word here is
the word sequence.

1007
00:58:58,574 --> 00:59:03,374
Namely, these events, these sets
that we're dealing with,

1008
00:59:03,374 --> 00:59:08,064
can be arranged so that we can
talk about the first event,

1009
00:59:08,064 --> 00:59:14,434
A1, the second event, A2, the
third one, A3, and so on.

1010
00:59:14,434 --> 00:59:18,454
To appreciate the issue that
arises here and to see why the

1011
00:59:18,454 --> 00:59:24,304
word sequence is so important,
let us consider the following

1012
00:59:24,304 --> 00:59:26,054
calculation.

1013
00:59:26,054 --> 00:59:28,624
Our sample space is
the unit square.

1014
00:59:28,624 --> 00:59:31,694


1015
00:59:31,694 --> 00:59:35,234
And we consider a model where
the probability of a set is

1016
00:59:35,234 --> 00:59:39,974
its area, as in the examples
that we considered earlier.

1017
00:59:39,974 --> 00:59:43,494
Let us now look at the
probability of the overall

1018
00:59:43,494 --> 00:59:45,124
sample space.

1019
00:59:45,124 --> 00:59:50,834
Our sample space is the unit
square and the unit square can

1020
00:59:50,834 --> 00:59:56,814
be thought of as the union of
various sets that consist of

1021
00:59:56,814 --> 00:59:58,274
single points.

1022
00:59:58,274 --> 01:00:05,724
So it's the union of subsets
with one element each.

1023
01:00:05,724 --> 01:00:08,044
And it's a union taken
over all the

1024
01:00:08,044 --> 01:00:11,714
points in the unit square.

1025
01:00:11,714 --> 01:00:14,534
Then we think about
additivity.

1026
01:00:14,534 --> 01:00:18,434
We observe that these subsets
are disjoint.

1027
01:00:18,434 --> 01:00:22,024
If we're considering different
points, then we get disjoint

1028
01:00:22,024 --> 01:00:23,834
single element sets.

1029
01:00:23,834 --> 01:00:27,134
And then an additivity property
would tells us that

1030
01:00:27,134 --> 01:00:30,394
the probability of these
union is the sum of the

1031
01:00:30,394 --> 01:00:36,694
probabilities of the different
single element subsets.

1032
01:00:36,694 --> 01:00:40,854
Now, as we discussed before,
single element subsets have 0

1033
01:00:40,854 --> 01:00:41,714
probability.

1034
01:00:41,714 --> 01:00:47,264
So we have a sum of lots of 0s
and the sum of 0s should be

1035
01:00:47,264 --> 01:00:49,254
equal to 0.

1036
01:00:49,254 --> 01:00:52,254
On the other hand, by the
probability axioms, the

1037
01:00:52,254 --> 01:00:54,804
probability of the entire
sample space

1038
01:00:54,804 --> 01:00:56,694
should be equal to 1.

1039
01:00:56,694 --> 01:01:01,084
And so we have established
that 1 is equal to 0.

1040
01:01:01,084 --> 01:01:03,064
This looks like a paradox.

1041
01:01:03,064 --> 01:01:04,784
Is it?

1042
01:01:04,784 --> 01:01:09,054
The catch is that there is
nothing in the axioms we have

1043
01:01:09,054 --> 01:01:12,714
introduced so far or the
properties we have established

1044
01:01:12,714 --> 01:01:15,544
that would justify this step.

1045
01:01:15,544 --> 01:01:19,884
So this step here
is questionable.

1046
01:01:19,884 --> 01:01:23,384
You might argue that the unit
square is the union of

1047
01:01:23,384 --> 01:01:28,434
disjoint single element sets,
which is the case that we have

1048
01:01:28,434 --> 01:01:30,284
in additivity axioms.

1049
01:01:30,284 --> 01:01:33,894
But the additivity axiom only
applies when we have a

1050
01:01:33,894 --> 01:01:36,714
sequence of events.

1051
01:01:36,714 --> 01:01:39,524
And this is not what
we have here.

1052
01:01:39,524 --> 01:01:42,414
This is not a union
of a sequence of

1053
01:01:42,414 --> 01:01:44,034
single element sets.

1054
01:01:44,034 --> 01:01:47,104
In fact, there is no way that
the elements of the unit

1055
01:01:47,104 --> 01:01:49,874
square can be arranged
in a sequence.

1056
01:01:49,874 --> 01:01:56,384
The unit square is said to
be an uncountable set.

1057
01:01:56,384 --> 01:01:59,894
This is a deep and fundamental
mathematical fact.

1058
01:01:59,894 --> 01:02:02,924
What it essentially says is that
there are two kinds of

1059
01:02:02,924 --> 01:02:04,454
infinite sets.

1060
01:02:04,454 --> 01:02:09,394
Discrete ones or in formal
terminology countable.

1061
01:02:09,394 --> 01:02:12,924
These are sets whose elements
can be arranged in a sequence,

1062
01:02:12,924 --> 01:02:14,624
like the integers.

1063
01:02:14,624 --> 01:02:19,854
And also uncountable sets, such
as the unit square or the

1064
01:02:19,854 --> 01:02:22,974
real line, whose elements
cannot be

1065
01:02:22,974 --> 01:02:25,084
arranged in a sequence.

1066
01:02:25,084 --> 01:02:28,624
If you're curious, you can
find the proof of this

1067
01:02:28,624 --> 01:02:31,344
important fact in the
supplementary materials that

1068
01:02:31,344 --> 01:02:33,964
we are providing.

1069
01:02:33,964 --> 01:02:36,624
After all these discussion, you
may now have legitimate

1070
01:02:36,624 --> 01:02:40,284
suspicions about the models
we have been looking at.

1071
01:02:40,284 --> 01:02:43,804
Is area a legitimate
probability law?

1072
01:02:43,804 --> 01:02:48,544
Does it even satisfy countable
additivity?

1073
01:02:48,544 --> 01:02:51,944
This question takes us into deep
waters and has to do with

1074
01:02:51,944 --> 01:02:55,194
a deep subfield of mathematics
called Measure Theory.

1075
01:02:55,194 --> 01:02:58,914
Fortunately, it turns out
that all is well.

1076
01:02:58,914 --> 01:03:01,974
Area is a legitimate
probability law.

1077
01:03:01,974 --> 01:03:06,544
It does indeed satisfy the
countable additivity axiom as

1078
01:03:06,544 --> 01:03:12,214
long as we only deal with nice
subsets of the unit square.

1079
01:03:12,214 --> 01:03:15,584
Fortunately, the subsets that
arise in whatever we do in

1080
01:03:15,584 --> 01:03:18,164
this course will be "nice".

1081
01:03:18,164 --> 01:03:22,834
Subsets that are not nice are
quite pathological and we will

1082
01:03:22,834 --> 01:03:25,204
not encounter them.

1083
01:03:25,204 --> 01:03:30,114
At this stage we are not in a
position to say anything more

1084
01:03:30,114 --> 01:03:33,144
that would be meaningful about
these issues because they're

1085
01:03:33,144 --> 01:03:36,174
quite complicated and
mathematically deep.

1086
01:03:36,174 --> 01:03:40,494
We can only say that there are
some serious mathematical

1087
01:03:40,494 --> 01:03:41,604
subtleties.

1088
01:03:41,604 --> 01:03:44,564
But fortunately, they
can all be overcome

1089
01:03:44,564 --> 01:03:46,134
in a rigorous manner.

1090
01:03:46,134 --> 01:03:49,134
And for the rest of this class,
you can just forget

1091
01:03:49,134 --> 01:03:50,654
about these subtle issues.

1092
01:03:50,654 --> 01:03:51,977


1093
01:03:52,004 --> 01:03:53,404


1094
01:03:53,404 --> 01:03:56,864
We end this lecture sequence
by stepping back to discuss

1095
01:03:56,864 --> 01:04:01,024
what probability theory really
is and what exactly is the

1096
01:04:01,024 --> 01:04:03,994
meaning of the word
probability.

1097
01:04:03,994 --> 01:04:06,994
In the most narrow view,
probability theory is just a

1098
01:04:06,994 --> 01:04:08,594
branch of mathematics.

1099
01:04:08,594 --> 01:04:10,594
We start with some axioms.

1100
01:04:10,594 --> 01:04:14,084
We consider models that satisfy
these axioms, and we

1101
01:04:14,084 --> 01:04:16,414
establish some consequences,
which are the

1102
01:04:16,414 --> 01:04:19,114
theorems of this theory.

1103
01:04:19,114 --> 01:04:22,734
You could do all that without
ever asking the question of

1104
01:04:22,734 --> 01:04:26,174
what the word "probability"
really means.

1105
01:04:26,174 --> 01:04:29,734
Yet, one of the theorems of
probability theory, that we

1106
01:04:29,734 --> 01:04:33,904
will see later in this class,
is that probabilities can be

1107
01:04:33,904 --> 01:04:38,254
interpreted as frequencies,
very loosely speaking.

1108
01:04:38,254 --> 01:04:42,124
If I have a fair coin, and I
toss it infinitely many times,

1109
01:04:42,124 --> 01:04:44,584
then the fraction of
heads that I will

1110
01:04:44,584 --> 01:04:47,174
observe will be one half.

1111
01:04:47,174 --> 01:04:50,884
In this sense, the probability
of an event, A, can be

1112
01:04:50,884 --> 01:04:55,164
interpreted as the frequency
with which event A will occur

1113
01:04:55,164 --> 01:04:59,884
in an infinite number of
repetitions of the experiment.

1114
01:04:59,884 --> 01:05:02,784
But is this all there is?

1115
01:05:02,784 --> 01:05:05,094
If we're dealing with coin
tosses, it makes sense to

1116
01:05:05,094 --> 01:05:07,414
think of probabilities
as frequencies.

1117
01:05:07,414 --> 01:05:12,234
But consider a statement such
as the "current president of

1118
01:05:12,234 --> 01:05:15,624
my country will be reelected
in the next election with

1119
01:05:15,624 --> 01:05:18,394
probability 0.7".

1120
01:05:18,394 --> 01:05:22,184
It's hard to think of this
number, 0.7, as a frequency.

1121
01:05:22,184 --> 01:05:25,024
It does not make sense to
think of infinitely many

1122
01:05:25,024 --> 01:05:27,794
repetitions of the
next election.

1123
01:05:27,794 --> 01:05:31,754
In cases like this, and in many
others, it is better to

1124
01:05:31,754 --> 01:05:35,214
think of probabilities
as just some way of

1125
01:05:35,214 --> 01:05:37,304
describing our beliefs.

1126
01:05:37,304 --> 01:05:40,924
And if you're a betting person,
probabilities can be

1127
01:05:40,924 --> 01:05:44,464
thought of as some numerical
guidance into what kinds of

1128
01:05:44,464 --> 01:05:48,784
bets you might be
willing to make.

1129
01:05:48,784 --> 01:05:53,444
But now if we think of
probabilities as beliefs, you

1130
01:05:53,444 --> 01:05:56,594
can run into the argument
that, well, beliefs are

1131
01:05:56,594 --> 01:05:57,824
subjective.

1132
01:05:57,824 --> 01:06:01,864
Isn't probability theory
supposed to be an objective

1133
01:06:01,864 --> 01:06:04,274
part of math and science?

1134
01:06:04,274 --> 01:06:08,264
Is probability theory just an
exercise in subjectivity?

1135
01:06:08,264 --> 01:06:10,544
Well, not quite.

1136
01:06:10,544 --> 01:06:12,254
There's more to it.

1137
01:06:12,254 --> 01:06:16,214
Probability, at the minimum,
gives us some rules for

1138
01:06:16,214 --> 01:06:21,314
thinking systematically about
uncertain situations.

1139
01:06:21,314 --> 01:06:24,454
And if it happens that our
probability model, our

1140
01:06:24,454 --> 01:06:28,384
subjective beliefs, have some
relation with the real world,

1141
01:06:28,384 --> 01:06:31,914
then probability theory can be
a very useful tool for making

1142
01:06:31,914 --> 01:06:37,004
predictions and decisions that
apply to the real world.

1143
01:06:37,004 --> 01:06:40,754
Now, whether your predictions
and decisions will be any good

1144
01:06:40,754 --> 01:06:45,124
will depend on whether you
have chosen a good model.

1145
01:06:45,124 --> 01:06:47,644
Have you chosen a model that's
provides a good enough

1146
01:06:47,644 --> 01:06:51,084
representation of
the real world?

1147
01:06:51,084 --> 01:06:53,664
How do you make sure that
this is the case?

1148
01:06:53,664 --> 01:06:57,024
There's a whole field, the field
of statistics, whose

1149
01:06:57,024 --> 01:07:01,274
purpose is to complement
probability theory by using

1150
01:07:01,274 --> 01:07:04,754
data to come up with
good models.

1151
01:07:04,754 --> 01:07:09,344
And so we have the following
diagram that summarizes the

1152
01:07:09,344 --> 01:07:12,204
relation between the real
world, statistics, and

1153
01:07:12,204 --> 01:07:13,254
probability.

1154
01:07:13,254 --> 01:07:15,924
The real world generates data.

1155
01:07:15,924 --> 01:07:19,234
The field of statistics and
inference uses these data to

1156
01:07:19,234 --> 01:07:21,684
come up with probabilistic
models.

1157
01:07:21,684 --> 01:07:24,724
Once we have a probabilistic
model, we use probability

1158
01:07:24,724 --> 01:07:28,394
theory and the analysis tools
that it provides to us.

1159
01:07:28,394 --> 01:07:32,364
And the results that we get
from this analysis lead to

1160
01:07:32,364 --> 01:07:34,654
predictions and decisions
about the real world.

1161
01:07:34,654 --> 01:07:39,605

